    <notes>Invocation of Calliope and Apollo
A hymn by Mesomedes of Crete, citharode at the court of Hadrian, c. 130 AD.
Source: M. L. West, Ancient Greek Music (Oxford 1992), no. 17, p. 303, in the
transcription a tone above the manuscript's pitch; K. Pohlmann, Denkmaeler
altgriechischer Musik (DAM), no. 2.

Kalliopeia sophā, Mousōn prokathāgeti terpnōn,
kai sophe mystodota, Lātous gone, Dēlie Paiān,
eumeneis pareste moi.
"Skilful Calliope, leader of the delightsome Muses, and skilful instructor,
son of Leto, Delian Paian, favour and be with me."

The manuscript gives pitch and rhythm. Our realisation choices: the tempo
(quarter = 80), the instruments, the intro and outro, and the reading of the
note in bar 4. West has a g-sharp there, here E-up (81/64, the Pythagorean
major third), the one note outside the scale; Winnington-Ingram reads g, here
E-flat. The song is bars 1-12 in 2/4 and bar 13 in 12/8; the 12/8 bar
is a time signature change in the arrangement, back to 2/4 for the outro. The
melismas (bar 4 E-up to C, bar 12 E-flat to F, bar 13 G to E-flat) are single
syllables, so the singer takes no breath inside them. The lyre plays the
melody, once an octave lower, and a drone on C and G at the start of each of
the three lines; the aulos (double reed-pipe, here an oboe) carries the sung
line in place of the voice: two pipes, the second a few cents sharp and a few
milliseconds late so they beat, with a breath before the second and third
lines and the pipes stopping after the last note; a low drone pipe on the
tonic; a frame drum (tympanon) marking the two pulses of each bar; small
cymbals (kymbala) at the start of each line and on the final chord; a two-bar
anabole (the kitharode's "striking up"): the lyre runs up its open strings and
settles on the fifth while the drones enter, and the singing begins at bar 3. A
two-bar outro echoes the cadence on the lyre and settles on the tonic and fifth
while the voice holds its last note, then stops. The tempo broadens over the
outro from 80 to 64 (ZT commands on the master track).

The scale is nid qablim, the Phrygian mode on C of the Pythagorean gamut:
C D-flat E-flat F G A-flat B-flat = 1/1 256/243 32/27 4/3 3/2 128/81 16/9. In
53-EDO's notation the gamut spells these C-sharp-up, E-flat-down, A-flat-down
and B-flat-down.</notes>
